  (comment List "The collection of all lists or finite sequences of items, as an abstract notion.  A #$List is a special kind of #$Tuple that is a finite structure with a first and last member-position, and for each member-postion but the last, there is a successor member-position.   A #$List, like other #$Tuples, allows repetition of its members -- the same item can appear at multiple member-positions in the list.  A list can be viewed formally as a function from a finite index set of counting numbers, beginning with one, into the domain of #$Things or perhaps some more restrictive domain.  Unlike #$Series, #$List is purely abstract and the only implied relation between an item and its successor is the successor relation of the list itself.  #$List is, technically, more specific than #$Tuple only in that the index set must be the counting numbers in their usual order rather than some other index set.  Note that the operators defined on lists do not correspond exactly to similar operators in Common LISP; #$List includes commonsense lists like grocery list and the first item on a list is at position number 1.")
)

