(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE 95222
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment IntelligenceProcess "#$IntelligenceProcess is a collection of events in which some agent
collects, analyzes, and disseminates information about some particular topic.  
Military intelligence is
a paradigmn example of such a process.  Such a process will often involve the
gathering of information from denied sources (popularly known as 'spying') but
it can also involve the collection of information from open sources.  It is the 
information resulting from an analysis of the raw data (e.g. satellite photos), 
rather than the raw data itself, that is an instance of
#$IntelligenceInformation.   The final step in an instance
of #$IntelligenceProcess is the dissemination of the resulting instances of
#$IntelligenceInformation  to
the agents for whom the information is being produced.  In general, we talk about
an intelligence process only in a setting in which the process has been
institutionalized, with particular positions in some organization (such as a
military intelligence unit or an analysis group within the C.I.A.) devoted to the
gathering, analysis, and dissemination of information.  We typically do not
refer to the gathering, analysis, and dissemination of information outside an
institutional context as an intelligence process - e.g., a private individual's
collection and analysis of information about various types of personal computers
would not count as an intelligence process.")
)

