(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment MathematicalOrdering "The collection of all those #$RelationalStructures that are called 'orderings'.  A #$MathematicalOrdering is usually described as an ordered pair <S, R> where S is a set and R is a binary relation on S that is transitive, i.e., for each X, Y and Z in S, R(X Y) and R(Y Z) imply R(X Z).  We do not have to define a #$MathematicalOrdering in Cyc as an ordered pair, but it is essential that each such #$MathematicalOrdering has a unique #$baseSet and a unique ordering relation.  We use, for each #$MathematicalOrdering ORDER, (#$orderingRelations ORDER PRED) to specify a binary predicate PRED, the restriction of which to the #$baseSet S of ORDER indicates the ordering relation R on S, and we require the collections that are used to specify the arguments to PRED to be supersets of S.  In principle, there could be different predicates PRED1 and PRED2 such that when restricted to the same #$baseSet S of a #$MathematicalOrdering ORDER, they order the elements of S exactly the same way.  When we said that there is a unique ordering relation R on S, we mean to ignore the difference between PRED1 and PRED2 when they are restricted to S, and treat the results of such restrictions the same, as far as they are used to talk about ORDER.  Subcollections of #$MathematicalOrdering include #$PartialOrdering, #$PartialOrdering-Strict, #$TreeOrdering, #$TreeOrdering-Strict, #$TotalOrdering, #$TotalOrdering-Strict, #$WellOrdering and #$Lattice-LatticeTheoretic, etc.")
)

(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :MONOTONIC :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment SpatialThing-Localized "This is the collection of all spatial things that can be meaningfully said to have location or position in the empirically observable Universe of the context in which the term in question is defined.  It includes both #$PartiallyTangible entities like pyramids and ships, and #$Intangible entities like the #$Equator.  The collection itself is agnostic with respect to the tangibility of its instances, although it is presumably inclusive of all #$PartiallyTangible entities.  #$Events that can be pinned down to specific places, such as #$PhysicalEvents, are also instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  However, many #$Events are non-examples; e.g., a law coming into effect.  Mental events are probably not examples--at least not in every #$Microtheory. Possible case of #$SpatialThing which are not cases #$SpatialThing-Localized: certain instances of #$TheTerm.  For example, if someone were to create (#$The #$Circle).  Also, all instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized have a temporally finite lifespan, the upper bound being the lifespan of space-time, or the lifespan of the space-time of the context of definition. Imaginary entities like Frodo, Captain Queeg, and #$HAL9000-TheComputer may be localized within the imaginary universes attaching the fictitious contexts in which they are defined, and so may be instances of #$SpatialThing-Localized within those microtheoretic contexts.")
)

