(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :MONOTONIC :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment frequencyOfActionType "A predicate for stating about types of #$TemporalThings the frequency
with which typical instances play certain roles in certain types of
events. Specifically, (#$frequencyOfActionType ACTTYPE ACTORTYPE ROLE
FREQUENCY) indicates that typical instances of ACTORTYPE play the role
ROLE in instances of ACTTYPE with the frequency FREQUENCY. Thus
#$frequencyOfActionType can be used to express the fact that typical
animals are virtually always breathing: (#$frequencyOfActionType
#$Breathing #$Vertebrate #$bodilyDoer #$Continuously). Even more
interesting examples of this predicate express the the rules of thumb
that in a modern Western personal hygiene context a typical person
engages in a toothbrushing event about one to three times a day, while
as the recipient of professional service that happens 0.5 - 4 times
per year. Those axioms would be written -- in the appropriate contexts
of course -- (frequencyOfActionType TeethCleaning Person performedBy
(TimesPerDay 1 3)) and (frequencyOfActionType TeethCleaning Person
recipientOfService (TimesPerYear 0.5 4)). This predicate should *not*
be used for action types that instances of ACTORTYPE do not typically
perform role ROLE in, even if those instances of ACTORTYPE that do, do
so with frequency FREQUENCY. For those kinds of assertions, use
#$regularFrequencyOfActionType. Also note that ACTTYPE must be a
collection of #$Situations [there is an argument constraint on this
predicate that enforces this, namely (#$arg1Genl
#$frequencyOfActionType #$Situation)].")
)

