(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment nextToLikeObjects "This predicate relates objects of the same type 
that are located next to or beside each other.  
(#$nextToLikeObjects OBJ1 OBJ2 COLL) means that OBJ1 and 
OBJ2 are both instances of COLL, and OBJ1 is spatially next 
to OBJ2.  More precisely: (i) OBJ1 and OBJ2 are both
instances of COLL, (ii) OBJ1 and OBJ2 are #$near each other,
(iii) the bottom of OBJ1 is not #$above-Generally the top 
of OBJ2, or vice versa, (iv) no other member of COLL is
#$between OBJ1 and OBJ2, and (v) no other member of 
COLL is both nearer to OBJ1 and in the same general 
direction from OBJ1 as OBJ2 is, or vice versa.  One person 
directly in front of another person in a queue, two houses 
that are next door to each other, and adjacent squares on a 
chessboard are all examples of pairs of objects related by 
#$nextToLikeObjects.  Two things can be related by 
#$nextToLikeObjects even though there is an _unlike_ object 
(i.e. something not an instance of COLL) between them.  For 
example, two houses might be #$nextToLikeObjects each other 
despite the fact that there is a fence, tree, or driveway 
between them.  When two objects stand in the relation
#$nextToLikeObjects with respect to some collection, it 
follows that there is some #$ArrangementOfLikeObjects
having at least those two objects as members.")
)

