 :KIF
  (comment typeBehaviorCapable "The predicate #$typeBehaviorCapable is used to describe a type of situation (and the role) in which objects of a certain type can participate due to their intrinsic properties.  (#$typeBehaviorCapable OBJ-TYPE SIT-TYPE ROLE) means that instances of the collection OBJ-TYPE are able to act as ROLE in a situation of type SIT-TYPE.  Instances of OBJ-TYPE may or may not have been designed to function in that way (cf. #$primaryFunction, #$intendedBehaviorCapable).  Also, #$typeBehaviorCapable does not imply that instances of OBJ-TYPE can unquestionably act in that ROLE in every situation of SIT-TYPE, because extrinsic factors may prevent that; e.g., an object may be in the wrong location, operated by a person lacking the proper skills, certain legal preconditions may not be satisfied, etc.  Examples: (1) an intended capability: (#$typeBehaviorCapable #$Hammer #$HammeringANail #$deviceUsed) means that hammers are capable of being the #$deviceUsed in instances of #$HammeringANail; (2) an unintended capability: (#$typeBehaviorCapable #$InnerTube #$FloatingInLiquid #$deviceUsed) means that inner tubes are capable of being the #$deviceUsed in instances of people #$FloatingInLiquid. Note: compare this predicate with the instance-level predicate #$behaviorCapable.")
)

