(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment quotedCollection "(#$quotedCollection COLLECTION) means that the members of the #$Collection COLLECTION are the Cyc terms themselves, rather than what those terms denote. For example, in the statement (#$isa #$IndianOcean #$PublicConstant), what is a member of the collection #$PublicConstant is the Cyc term #$IndianOcean, not what that term denotes, which isn't a Cyc constant, but an ocean.  This is true of all of the members of the collection #$PublicConstant, so we should assert (#$quotedCollection #$PublicConstant).  On the other hand, in the statement (#$isa #$IndianOcean #$Ocean), it is what the Cyc term denotes--the ocean--which is a member of the collection of all oceans. The cyc term itself is not a member of this collection, since it isn't an ocean, but a constant.  This is often called the 'use versus mention' distinction. In the statement 'Bertrand Russell was a philosopher,' one is using the term 'Bertrand Russell', whereas in the statement ' 'Bertrand Russell' is a proper noun,' one is mentioning the term 'Bertrand Russell.' In English, in order to indicate that one is mentioning rather than using a term, the term is often enclosed in quote marks: hence the name of our predicate. See also #$quotedArgument.")
)

