(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment physicalReferentOf "This binary predicate is used to assert that a particular
#$PartiallyTangible object or #$PhysicalEvent is the 'physical
referent' used to 'pin down' a particular #$SpatialThing. If a
#$SpatialThing 'exists' in the empirically observable universe, i.e.,
if it is a #$SpatialThing-Localized, then it must be localized in
virtue either of some physical reference point or of some other
#$Intangible #$SpatialThing-Localized.  A spatial system, like the
#$GeodeticCoordinateSystem, must ultimately 'ground out' in 'physical
entities' that are either #$PartiallyTangible, like the
#$RoyalObservatory-Greenwich, or that are #$PhysicalEvents, like the
#$RotationOfEarth.  This predicate is very broad: it could, for
example, be used to represent the case where a boundary stone is used
to mark a property boundary, the case where the rotation of the earth
localizes the geographic poles, and the case of the
#$RoyalObservatory-Greenwich being used to 'ground' the
#$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.  Borderline cases include cases where
borders are instantiated by physical objects, the way the Rio Grande
instantiates part of the US-Mexico border or the way the ridge of
Everest instantiates part of the Nepal-Tibetan border.  Non-examples
include cases where one #$Intangible #$SpatialThing-Localized is used
to localize another: for example, the case where the geographic poles
are used to localize the #$Equator.")
)

