  (comment oppositeOnPath-Generally "This predicate relates a customary path such as a 
street to two objects that are generally across the path from 
each other.  (#$oppositeOnPath-Generally OBJ1 OBJ2 PATH) means 
that some part of OBJ1 is directly across PATH from some part
of OBJ2.  In other words, OBJ1 and OBJ2 are #$onPath-Alongside 
opposite sides of PATH, and the two segments of PATH that OBJ1 
and OBJ2 are respectively related to by #$alongsidePathSegment 
spatially intersect.  More precisely: there is some vertical 
plane perpendicular to the vertical ''plane'' defined by the 
centerline of PATH (or, even more precisely, by the centerline
of the portion of PATH common to the two segments of PATH 
mentioned above) that passes through both OBJ1 and OBJ2.  For 
a specialization of this predicate that implies that the 
objects are more directly across the path from each other, 
see #$oppositeOnPath-Directly.")
)

