(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE NIL
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment NoteAboutSidedObject "We consider this concept to be a lynchpin in the CycL representation
of direction.  A #$BilateralObject is an object which is conventionally
considered to have either a top and a bottom, a front and a back, or a
left and a right.  These possibilities are each represented with a spec.

Here are some examples and some non-examples of members of this 
category.

A perfectly smooth ball bearing is usually (read on for exceptions) a
negative exemplar of a #$BilateralObject.  On the other hand a ball
bearing with a dot painted on one side, could be considered to have a
well-defined front if, for example, the dotted were designated as the
front side.

A typical water glass has a top and bottom and no left and right nor
front and back.  Thus it is strictly a #$BilateralObject and not a
#$HexalateralObject.

A javelin has a front and back and no left and right nor top and
bottom.  Thus it is strictly a #$BilateralObject, and not a
#$HexalateralObject.

Objects that typically move in some direction such as vehicles,
mammals, commets, or cars have their fronts and backs determined by
their typical direction of motion.

In #$ModernShelterConstructions, 'front' and 'back' are generally
determined by which side faces the street, and 'top' and 'bottom' by
which side 'normally' is in the air and which side 'normally' is 
on the ground.  From these four an intrinsic left and right can
also be established.

We do not distinguish very many objects in the everyday world that are
#$BilateralObjects with respect to left and right and not with respect
to anything else.  In most cases--including automobiles, houses, and
human beings--left and right exist solely because there is a
well-established top, bottom, front, and back.  This seems to be a
peculiarity of human psychology.

As regards #$TopAndBottomSidedObject, #$LeftAndRightSidedObject, and
#$FrontAndBackSidedObject, we should be VERY careful to include as specs of
these collections only types for which there are already
well-established conventions for distinguishing the sides in question.
It is probably the case that any instance of #$PartiallyTangible is
POTENTIALLY a #$HexalateralObject, or at least a #$BilateralObject of
some sort--i.e., we could invent conventions for distinguishing the
relevant sides.  The possibility of such invention is not a sufficient
condition for membership in any of the 'bilateral' collections.  For
example, in most microtheories, the collection of ball bearing should
not #$BilateralObject as a #$genls.

Here are some potentially misleading non-examples: 

Hourglass: no conventions regarding any sides 

Book: no clear-cut conventions regarding any sides--if it lies face-up
on the table the 'front' of the book may be the front cover; in a
bookcase, the 'front' may well be the spine.  In other words although
an object may have front, back, etc sides determined by considerations
extrinsic to the object (such as its relationship to the bookshelf)
#$HexalateralObjects have front, back, etc sides determined
*intrinsically*.

Desk: obvious convention for distinguishing top from bottom; no
convention for distinguishing front and back: it depends which side of
the desk you're on.  However, desks designed to be against a wall do
have clear front and back sides.

Note that mere assymetry does not  make something a #$BilateralObject.
Consider,  for  example,  a  nebula  or  a  crumpled  piece  of paper.
Although they are assymetric on all three  axis, they are not elements
of #$HexalateralObject  since no  conventions exist for distinguishing
an  intrinsic left, right, bottom,  top, front,  or back.  Conversely,
take  an  essentially   symmetrical object    like  an  aluminum  can.
Notwithstanding   its  overall   symmetry,   there  exist  established
conventions according  to which the side  on which it 'normally' rests
is its bottom  and  the side with  the  pulltab is its top.   Likewise
there are established conventions according to which the side with the
brand  name on it  is the 'front' and  the side opposite that side the
back, and with  the aid of these we  can distinguish an intrinsic left
and right for the can as well.

Note that any two axes determine the third.  Thus any object which is
both a #$LeftAndRightSidedObject and a #$FrontAndBackSidedObject is
also a #$TopAndBottomSidedObject.")
)

