(:DIRECTION :FORWARD :MONOTONICITY :DEFAULT :CREATOR Cyc :CREATION-DATE 140525
 :MICROTHEORY BaseKB
 :KIF
  (comment followingValue "The Cyc predicate #$followingValue is used to represent an ordering of generic attributes.  (#$followingValue VAL1 VAL2) means that VAL2 is a greater value than VAL1 on a scale that they share.  (#$followingValue VAL1 VAL2) implies (#$greaterThan VAL2 VAL1).  Typically, one uses #$followingValue when the values VAL1 and VAL2 are elements of #$GenericQuantity, and they are not grounded in numerically quantifiable units of measure (see #$NoteAboutGivingGenericValueFunctionsNumericValues).  If VAL1 and VAL2 do have numerical grounding, a #$followingValue formula is unnecessary since #$numericallyEqual, #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, and #$greaterThan, are automatically computable using arithmetic relations holding between the minimum and maximum of val1 and val2.  (See #$minQuantValue, #$maxQuantValue.)  For example, one must use #$followingValue to state that (#$HighAmountFn #$Glamor) is more glamorous than (#$LowAmountFn #$Glamor).  On the other hand, it is unnecessary to assert a #$followingValue relation between (#$MilesPerHour 5) and (#$MilesPerHour 25 60) since #$greaterThan is arithmetically determinable.  See also #$GenericQuantity, #$GenericValueFunction.")
)

